<Layout title="Free Forex Trading Tools & Risk Calculators" description="Free forex trade-risk tools from TRADE90. Start with our forex and gold position size calculator: understand risk, lot size and stop-loss assumptions before placing a trade." canonical="/tools/">
<main class="wrap">
<header class="page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">TRADING TOOLS · RISK MANAGEMENT</span><h1>Free Forex Trading Tools</h1><p>Make position sizing a deliberate decision. TRADE90's free tools complement our monetary-policy research without offering trade signals or pretending to know your broker's live prices.</p></header>
<section class="tool-grid" aria-label="Available trading tools"><a class="tool" href="/tools/position-size-calculator/"><span class="eyebrow">FREE CALCULATOR</span><h2>Forex &amp; Gold Position Size Calculator</h2><p>Enter a USD account balance, risk percentage and stop-loss distance to calculate an indicative standard-lot size. Supports 28 FX pairs and XAU/USD with manual conversion where necessary.</p><strong>Calculate your position size →</strong></a></section>
<section class="info"><h2>Risk before forecasts</h2><p>Even a well-researched forex view can lead to a large loss if the position size is too big. Start with a cash-risk budget, define the stop-loss distance, and check whether the modeled loss fits that budget. Costs, gaps, contract differences and leverage may increase your actual loss.</p><p>After sizing the trade, explore <a href="/pairs/">TRADE90's currency-pair research</a> for independent, source-linked monetary-policy context, or read the <a href="/disclaimer/">risk disclaimer</a>.</p></section>
<section class="info"><h2>Before using a sizing result</h2><p>Check the instrument, account currency, contract size and stop-distance units. The calculator uses a USD account and conventional standard-lot assumptions. Cross-currency pairs need a conversion input, while gold uses a dollar price move per troy ounce. Your broker may specify a different contract size or minimum trade increment.</p><p>Review the modeled cash loss after entering costs. A small numerical result is not proof that a trade is appropriate, and an order may execute differently during gaps or thin liquidity. Keep the research question separate from the size calculation: policy-rate comparisons describe monetary conditions, while position sizing models exposure under the inputs you provide.</p></section>
</main></Layout>
